   241	### 5.2 Fixture manifests — excluded by a data rule, and audibly so
   242	
   243	`dev/release/fixtures/cargo-skew/**` (4 × `Cargo.toml`) and `dev/release/fixtures/pip-skew/**`
   244	(4 × `setup.py`) are **deliberately fake, deliberately skewed** manifests that exist to make the
   245	release version-skew gates demonstrate their catch. A naive "everything tracked" scope emits them as
   246	real dependencies, and every downstream consumer then sees phantom packages at wrong versions.
   247	
   248	**This is not a fresh decision — it re-states a ruling already on disk.** `.github/dependabot.yml`'s
   249	header carries an explicit *"Deliberately NOT covered (do NOT add — adding any of these reintroduces
   250	noise/orphans)"* list, and its third entry is verbatim:
   251	
   252	```text
   253	#   - dev/release/fixtures/cargo-skew/**  (intentional mock crates for release-skew tests).
   254	```
   255	
   256	The repository has therefore **already ruled**, under the reconciliation that closed Library Sweep #1
   257	(`0.8.11.1`, `R-SW-5`), that these manifests are out of dependency scope — and it ruled it for the
   258	same stated reason (noise / orphans). Excluding them here is *reconciling with* that ruling, not
   259	re-litigating it, which is what makes the decision cheap. The tool extends it in one direction the
   260	Dependabot config could not: `dev/release/fixtures/pip-skew/**` (the 4 `setup.py`) is not named
   300	
   301	**Free consequence.** Because the only tracked `setup.py` files are the four excluded pip-skew
   302	fixtures, the tool needs **no `setup.py` execution or AST parsing** — the single most fragile parser
   303	in the Python packaging space is never written. If a real `setup.py` is ever tracked it is
   304	discovered, hits no tier rule, and fails loudly (REQ-4) rather than being mis-parsed.
   305	
   306	### 5.3 Tier assignment, and the loud failure for a new manifest
   307	
   308	**The tier partition is derived from `.github/dependabot.yml`, not invented here.** That file is a
   309	tracked source of record whose header already partitions this repository along the same axis the
   310	tier vocabulary does — it names the entries that *"cover every SHIPPED/tracked manifest"*, and then
   311	names, explicitly, what is *"Deliberately NOT covered (do NOT add …)"*. Deriving `tiers.toml` from it
   312	means `shipped` / `dev-tooling` / `eval-only` reconcile with an existing ruling instead of a second
   313	opinion drifting away from it:
   314	
   315	| `dependabot.yml` entry / exclusion | Verbatim rationale in that file | Tier rule it yields |
   316	|---|---|---|
   317	| `pip /src/python` | "shipped Python package" | `src/python/` → `shipped` |
   318	| `cargo /` | "workspace root — covers all `src/rust/crates/*` members" | `Cargo.` + `src/rust/crates/` → `shipped` |
   319	| `npm /src/ts` | "TypeScript binding" | `src/ts/` → `shipped` |
   320	| `npm /` | "root markdown dev tooling — see `R-DEP-2` note" | `package` → `dev-tooling` |
   321	| `github-actions /` | "all `.github/workflows/*`" | **out of BOM scope** — no ecosystem in `{cargo, npm, pypi}`, no manifest basename; recorded here so its absence is deliberate, not an omission |
   322	| NOT covered: `/python` | "uv-managed **EVAL ENV**; `python/uv.lock` is gitignored, not shipped … the source of the idna/torch security-alert noise — keeping it uncovered is intentional" | out of scope by **untracked-ness** (§5.1), which is the stronger mechanism: `git ls-files` cannot reach it at all |
   323	| NOT covered: `dev/research/**`, `data/corpus-data/**` `requirements*.txt` | "eval/corpus fetch artifacts" | the `eval-only` tier exists for exactly these; all are gitignored today, which is why it currently matches nothing |
   324	| NOT covered: `dev/release/fixtures/cargo-skew/**` | "intentional mock crates for release-skew tests" | the `action = "exclude"` / `reason = "fixture"` rule (§5.2) |
   325	
   326	Two things fall out of reading it, and both are worth stating plainly.
   327	
   328	**(a) The fixture exclusion is the already-ruled position** (§5.2), which strengthens it
   329	considerably: this design is not asking for a new carve-out, it is honouring one that has been on
   330	disk since `0.8.11.1`.
   331	
   332	**(b) `dev/tools/mermaid/package.json` is a real, tracked npm manifest covered by NO Dependabot
   333	directory** — the configured `npm` entries are `/src/ts` and `/` only. That is a genuine coverage
   334	gap of the **same class as the `R-DEP-2` note** in that file, which records that the root
   335	`package-lock.json` carried two live advisories (`markdown-it` GHSA-6v5v-wf23-fmfq, `js-yaml`
   336	GHSA-h67p-54hq-rp68) while sitting under no configured directory, so its security PRs never
   337	auto-opened. `dev/tools/mermaid/` is in that position today.
   338	
   339	This design **rules nothing** about it — no manifest and no config is edited in 0.8.20 (§2), and
   340	`.github/**` is out of bounds for every leg. What matters here is that **the tool surfaces it
   341	mechanically**: `dev/tools/mermaid/package.json` and `dev/tools/mermaid/package-lock.json` are two of
   342	the 29 discovered paths, they tier as `dev-tooling`, and the set-difference against the five
   343	configured Dependabot directories is a one-line consumer of that output. Since LBS's own Definition
   344	of Done includes "`dependabot.yml` reconciled" (§1), that is a concrete, already-cashable
   345	demonstration of the tool's value: the manual sweep had this gap and did not report it. It is
   346	recorded here and handed to **Slice 33's §4 hand-off to 0.8.22**, which owns config changes.
   347	
   348	**`scripts/sbom-survey/tiers.toml`** (Slice 32 artifact; shape frozen here):
   349	
   350	```toml
   351	schema = 1
   352	
   353	[[rule]]
   354	prefix = "dev/release/fixtures/"
   355	action = "exclude"
   356	reason = "fixture"
   357	note   = "deliberately fake/skewed release-gate fixtures; not real dependencies"
   358	
   359	[[rule]]
   360	prefix = "src/rust/crates/"
   361	action = "tier"
   362	tier   = "shipped"
   363	
   364	[[rule]]
   365	prefix = "src/python/"
   366	action = "tier"
   367	tier   = "shipped"
   368	
   369	[[rule]]
   370	prefix = "src/ts/"
   371	action = "tier"
   372	tier   = "shipped"
   373	
   374	[[rule]]
   375	prefix = "Cargo."
   376	action = "tier"
   377	tier   = "shipped"
   378	note   = "root workspace manifest + lockfile"
   379	
   380	[[rule]]
   381	prefix = "package"
   382	action = "tier"
   383	tier   = "dev-tooling"
   384	note   = "root package.json/package-lock.json — name is literally fathomdb-dev-tooling, private, not published"
   385	
   386	[[rule]]
   387	prefix = "dev/tools/"
   388	action = "tier"
   389	tier   = "dev-tooling"
   390	
   391	[[rule]]
   392	prefix = "tools/docs/"
   393	action = "tier"
   394	tier   = "dev-tooling"
   395	
   396	[[rule]]
   397	prefix = "scripts/sbom-survey/"
   398	action = "tier"
   399	tier   = "dev-tooling"
   400	note   = "this tool surveys itself"
   401	```
   402	
   403	**Matching is longest-prefix-wins, not first-match-wins.** File order is then irrelevant, so
   404	reordering the rules can never change the answer — a property worth having in a file that will be
   405	edited by whoever adds the next manifest. Duplicate prefixes are a load-time error.
   406	
   407	**And that promise is now graded, because nothing enforced it** (codex §9 round 3, §12). The rules
   408	above happen to list every more-specific prefix ahead of the prefix that contains it, so a
   409	first-match-wins implementation satisfies every *other* criterion — and then a later edit that merely
   410	**moves a block** silently re-tiers manifests, in a file whose whole point is that moving a block is
   411	safe. `AC-SBOM-23` grades it on an **overlapping pair chosen so the two strategies disagree**:
   412	`dev/tools/` → `dev-tooling` against the longer `dev/tools/vendored-shipped/` → `shipped`. Neither
   413	path need exist on disk; `classify()` is a pure function of the rule set and the path string.
   414	
   415	| Obligation | What is asserted | What it catches |
   416	|---|---|---|
   417	| Longest wins | `dev/tools/vendored-shipped/Cargo.toml` → `shipped` | first-match-wins, in the order that puts the broad rule first |
   418	| Longest **matching**, not longest present | `dev/tools/mermaid/package.json` → `dev-tooling` | "answer with the longest rule in the map" — which a single-path test would pass |
   419	| Order-independence | the **same two rules in both orders** classify **identically** | any order-sensitive strategy: it must fail one of the two orderings |
   420	| Duplicates rejected at **load** | `load_tier_map()` on a file with one prefix twice **raises**, and the message **names the prefix** | a silent last-wins/first-wins resolution of a duplicate, which is the same ambiguity by another route |
   421	
   422	The exception **type** for a duplicate is deliberately not frozen here — the criterion asserts that
   423	the load fails and that the message names the offending prefix, plus the one negative constraint that
   424	it is **not** `UntieredManifestError`, since that error means "no rule matched this path" and no path
   425	has been classified yet when a rule file is rejected.
   426	
   427	**There is deliberately NO catch-all / default rule.** A tracked manifest matched by no rule raises
   428	`UntieredManifestError(path)`; `run_survey` propagates it and the CLI exits `2` with the path on
   429	stderr. A default would convert "somebody added a manifest and nobody classified it" — the exact
   430	event this tool exists to catch — into a silent mis-tag. This is `AC-SBOM-07` (unit) and
   431	`AC-SBOM-21` (CLI), and it is the failure mode that gets its own named test per §5 item 3.
   432	
   433	**`eval-only` currently matches nothing.** The eval/corpus trees (`data/corpus-data/**`,
   434	`dev/research/**` and `eval/**` lockfiles) are gitignored today, so no tracked manifest is
   435	eval-only — and those are precisely the trees `dependabot.yml` lists as deliberately uncovered. The
   436	value stays in the vocabulary because if one ever *becomes* tracked it must be taggable as eval-only
   437	rather than silently absorbed into `shipped`; the file's own note that `/python` "is the source of
   438	the idna/torch security-alert noise" is the concrete record of what mis-tagging an eval tree costs.
   439	
   440	### 5.4 The published-version seam — injectable, and honest when offline
   441	
   442	```python
   443	class PublishedVersionSource(Protocol):
   444	    def latest(self, ecosystem: str, name: str) -> str | None: ...
   445	```
   446	
   447	| Implementation | Behaviour | Used by |
   448	|---|---|---|
   449	| `OfflineSource()` | always returns `None` | default posture, `--offline`, whole test suite |
   450	| `StaticSource({(eco, name): version})` | returns the mapping, `None` if absent | tests (`AC-SBOM-17`) |
   451	| `HttpRegistrySource(timeout=…)` | `urllib.request` GET against crates.io / npm registry / PyPI JSON; **any** exception → `None` plus a recorded `lookup_error` | Slice 33 online run only |
   452	
   453	**The full `run_survey` signature**, since three criteria now inject through it:
   454	
   455	```python
   456	def run_survey(
   457	    repo_root: Path,
   458	    *,
   459	    published: PublishedVersionSource,   # REQUIRED, no default  — §5.4
   460	    tier_map: TierMap | None = None,     # None => load the tracked tiers.toml — §5.2
   730	
   731	### 5.9 CLI contract
   732	
   733	```text
   734	sbom-survey --repo PATH [--offline | --online] [--out DIR] [--tiers FILE] [--now ISO8601]
   735	sbom-survey --describe
   736	```
   737	
   738	| Exit | Meaning |
   739	|---|---|
   740	| `0` | survey written |
   741	| `2` | a tracked manifest has no tier assignment (path named on stderr) — REQ-4 |
   742	| `3` | a tracked manifest could not be parsed |
   743	| `1` | unexpected internal error |
   744	
   745	`--describe` prints `{"name": "sbom-survey", "ci_gating": false, "recurring": true, "tiers": [...]}`
   746	— the machine-readable form of "this is informational" (`AC-SBOM-19`).
